{"type":"final","text":"The field of machine learning has evolved dramatically over the past decade. What once required enormous computing clusters and specialized hardware can now run efficiently on consumer laptops and even mobile phones. Neural networks, once considered a niche curiosity, have become the backbone of modern artificial intelligence systems, ranging from voice assistants to medical diagnostics. The transformer architecture in particular has proven remarkably versatile, enabling breakthroughs natural language processing, computer vision, and even protein structure prediction. Researchers continue to push the boundaries of what is possible, exploring techniques like reinforcement learning from human feedback, which allows models to align more closely with human values and preferences. As these systems become more capable, questions around safety, interpretability, and responsible deployment become increasingly important for the broader research community."}
